t": " short"}, "request_id": "360a4f64-9675-4159-9d43-3fad76936f2f"}, {"ts": "2026-11-14T16:43:05.603008Z", "seq": 185, "node": "generate", "type": "token", "payload": {"text": "-term"}, "request_id": "360a4f64-9675-4159-9d43-3fad76936f2f"}, {"ts": "2026-11-14T16:43:05.642053Z", "seq": 186, "node": "generate", "type": "token", "payload": {"text": " memory"}, "request_id": "360a4f64-9675-4159-9d43-3fad76936f2f"}, {"ts": "2026-11-14T16:43:05.642053Z", "seq": 187, "node": "generate", "type": "token", "payload": {"text": " and"}, "request_id": "360a4f64-9675-4159-9d43-3fad76936f2f"}, {"ts": "2026-11-14T16:43:05.688532Z", "seq": 188, "node": "generate", "type": "token", "payload": {"text": " state"}, "request_id": "360a4f64-9675-4159-9d43-3fad76936f2f"}, {"ts": "2026-11-14T16:43:05.688532Z", "seq": 189, "node": "generate", "type": "token", "payload": {"text": " management"}, "request_id": "360a4f64-9675-4159-9d43-3fad76936f2f"}, {"ts": "2026-11-14T16:43:05.708603Z", "seq": 190, "node": "generate", "type": "token", "payload": {"text": " scoped"}, "request_id": "360a4f64-9675-4159-9d43-3fad76936f2f"}, {"ts": "2026-11-14T16:43:05.708603Z", "seq": 191, "node": "generate", "type": "token", "payload": {"text": " to"}, "request_id": "360a4f64-9675-4159-9d43-3fad76936f2f"}, {"ts": "2026-11-14T16:43:05.720640Z", "seq": 192, "node": "generate", "type": "token", "payload": {"text": " that"}, "request_id": "360a4f64-9675-4159-9d43-3fad76936f2f"}, {"ts": "2026-11-14T16:43:05.721650Z", "seq": 193, "node": "generate", "type": "token", "payload": {"text": " conversation"}, "request_id": "360a4f64-9675-4159-9d43-3fad76936f2f"}, {"ts": "2026-11-14T16:43:05.733652Z", "seq": 194, "node": "generate", "type": "token", "payload": {"text": " ["}, "request_id": "360a4f64-9675-4159-9d43-3fad76936f2f"}, {"ts": "2026-11-14T16:43:05.733652Z", "seq": 195, "node": "generate", "type": "token", "payload": {"text": "1"}, "request_id": "360a4f64-9675-4159-9d43-3fad76936f2f"}, {"ts": "2026-11-14T16:43:05.752155Z", "seq": 196, "node": "generate", "type": "token", "payload": {"text": "]["}, "request_id": "360a4f64-9675-4159-9d43-3fad76936f2f"}, {"ts": "2026-11-14T16:43:05.752155Z", "seq": 197, "node": "generate", "type": "token", "payload": {"text": "3"}, "request_id": "360a4f64-9675-4159-9d43-3fad76936f2f"}, {"ts": "2026-11-14T16:43:05.772081Z", "seq": 198, "node": "generate", "type": "token", "payload": {"text": "]["}, "request_id": "360a4f64-9675-4159-9d43-3fad76936f2f"}, {"ts": "2026-11-14T16:43:05.773076Z", "seq": 199, "node": "generate", "type": "token", "payload": {"text": "4"}, "request_id": "360a4f64-9675-4159-9d43-3fad76936f2f"}, {"ts": "2026-11-14T16:43:05.789355Z", "seq": 200, "node": "generate", "type": "token", "payload": {"text": "]["}, "request_id": "360a4f64-9675-4159-9d43-3fad76936f2f"}, {"ts": "2026-11-14T16:43:05.789355Z", "seq": 201, "node": "generate", "type": "token", "payload": {"text": "5"}, "request_id": "360a4f64-9675-4159-9d43-3fad76936f2f"}, {"ts": "2026-11-14T16:43:05.813638Z", "seq": 202, "node": "generate", "type": "token", "payload": {"text": "]."}, "request_id": "360a4f64-9675-4159-9d43-3fad76936f2f"}, {"ts": "2026-11-14T16:43:05.816639Z", "seq": 203, "node": "generate", "type": "node_end", "payload": {"model": "gpt-4.1-mini", "cost_usd": 0.0008088, "tokens_in": 1226, "latency_ms": 2947.235300001921, "tokens_out": 199, "tokens_cached": 0}, "request_id": "360a4f64-9675-4159-9d43-3fad76936f2f"}, {"ts": "2026-11-14T16:43:05.817637Z", "seq": 204, "node": null, "type": "done", "payload": {"answer": "A `thread_id` in LangGraph is a unique identifier assigned to each checkpoint saved by a checkpointer. It represents the accumulated state of a sequence of runs within a conversation or session. The `thread_id` scopes the conversation, including message history and checkpoints, allowing the system to persist and retrieve the state of the underlying graph of the assistant. It is essential for saving state, resuming execution after interrupts, and managing conversation history and other stateful data. When invoking a graph with a checkpointer, specifying a `thread_id` is mandatory to enable state persistence and retrieval. The `thread_id` is also used to correlate supervisor orchestration traces with subagent execution traces in async subagent runs. It is independent from per-run context data, which can be passed separately. Overall, `thread_id` organizes multiple interactions in a session, similar to grouping messages in an email conversation, enabling short-term memory and state management scoped to that conversation [1][3][4][5].", "citations": [{"n": 1, "url": "https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/deepagents/async-subagents#trace-with-thread-ids", "title": "Async subagents", "section": "Best practices > Trace with thread IDs", "chunk_id": "doc-deepagents-async-subagents#chunk-21"}, {"n": 3, "url": "https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/deepagents/going-to-production#invoking-the-agent", "title": "Going to production", "section": "Production considerations > Invoking the agent", "chunk_id": "doc-deepagents-going-to-production#chunk-11"}, {"n": 4, "url": "https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/checkpointers#threads", "title": "Checkpointers", "section": "Core concepts > Threads", "chunk_id": "doc-langgraph-checkpointers#chunk-2"}, {"n": 5, "url": "https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/concepts/memory#short-term-memory", "title": "Memory overview", "section": "Short-term memory", "chunk_id": "doc-concepts-memory#chunk-1"}], "total_cost_usd": 0.00080902, "total_latency_ms": 6674.312400005874, "counterfactual_cost_usd": null}, "request_id": "360a4f64-9675-4159-9d43-3fad76936f2f"}], "chunks": [{"id": "doc-concepts-memory#chunk-1", "doc_id": "doc-concepts-memory", "title": "Memory overview", "section": "Short-term memory", "url": "https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/concepts/memory#short-term-memory", "text": "[Short-term memory](/oss/langgraph/add-memory#add-short-term-memory) lets your application remember previous interactions within a single [thread](/oss/langgraph/checkpointers#threads) or conversation. A [thread](/oss/langgraph/checkpointers#threads) organizes multiple interactions in a session, similar to the way email groups messages in a single conversation.\n\nLangGraph manages short-term memory as part of the agent's state, persisted via thread-scoped checkpoints. This state can normally include the conversation history along with other stateful data, such as uploaded files, retrieved documents, or generated artifacts. By storing these in the graph's state, the bot can access the full context for a given conversation while maintaining separation between different threads."}, {"id": "doc-deepagents-async-subagents#chunk-21", "doc_id": "doc-deepagents-async-subagents", "title": "Async subagents", "section": "Best practices > Trace with thread IDs", "url": "https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/deepagents/async-subagents#trace-with-thread-ids", "text": "When using LangGraph-based deployments, every async subagent run is a standard LangGraph run, fully visible in LangSmith. The supervisor's trace shows tool calls for `launch`, `check`, `update`, `cancel`, and `list`. Each subagent run appears as a separate trace, linked by thread ID. Use the thread ID (task ID) to correlate supervisor orchestration traces with subagent execution traces."}, {"id": "doc-deepagents-going-to-production#chunk-11", "doc_id": "doc-deepagents-going-to-production", "title": "Going to production", "section": "Production considerations > Invoking the agent", "url": "https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/deepagents/going-to-production#invoking-the-agent", "text": "When deploying with the LangGraph SDK, the SDK manages threads for you and you pass the returned `thread_id` to each run:\n\n```python\nfrom langgraph_sdk import get_client\n\nclient = get_client(url=\"<DEPLOYMENT_URL>\", api_key=\"<LANGSMITH_API_KEY>\")\n\nthread = await client.threads.create()\nasync for chunk in client.runs.stream(\n    thread[\"thread_id\"],  # [!code highlight]\n    \"agent\",\n    input={\"messages\": [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Plan a 3-day trip to Tokyo\"}]},\n    context={\"user_id\": \"user-123\"},  # [!code highlight]\n    stream_mode=\"updates\",\n):\n    print(chunk.data)\n```\n\n<Tip>\n`thread_id` scopes the *conversation* (message history, checkpoints). `context` carries *per-run* data your tools and middleware read. They are independent: changing one does not affect the other, and you can pass either or both.\n</Tip>"}, {"id": "doc-langgraph-checkpointers#chunk-2", "doc_id": "doc-langgraph-checkpointers", "title": "Checkpointers", "section": "Core concepts > Threads", "url": "https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/checkpointers#threads", "text": "A thread is a unique ID or thread identifier assigned to each checkpoint saved by a checkpointer. It contains the accumulated state of a sequence of [runs](/langsmith/runs). When a run is executed, the [state](/oss/langgraph/graph-api#state) of the underlying graph of the assistant will be persisted to the thread.\n\nWhen invoking a graph with a checkpointer, you **must** specify a `thread_id` as part of the `configurable` portion of the config:\n\n```python\n{\"configurable\": {\"thread_id\": \"1\"}}\n```\n\nA thread's current and historical state can be retrieved. To persist state, a thread must be created prior to executing a run. The LangSmith API provides several endpoints for creating and managing threads and thread state. See the [API reference](https://reference.langchain.com/python/langsmith/) for more details.\n\nThe checkpointer uses `thread_id` as the primary key for storing and retrieving checkpoints. Without it, the checkpointer cannot save state or resume execution after an [interrupt](/oss/langgraph/interrupts), since the checkpointer uses `thread_id` to load the saved state."}]}